  "body": "The Upper Peninsula regions show minimal color change at seven percent, while the Lower Peninsula displays stark contrasts. Southwest Lower and Southeast Lower lead dramatically at ninety-seven and ninety-nine percent respectively, in falling and peak phases. Central and Saginaw Bay sits at forty-one percent falling. The remaining Lower Peninsula regions range from nine to seven percent in early decline.\n\nThis weekend's mixed skies across most regions favor viewing, though Western U.P. and Tip of the Mitt face some rain with cooler temperatures in the mid-sixties. Northeast Lower and Northwest Lower offer mixed conditions with highs near sixty-six degrees. Southeast Lower provides the most stable weather at seventy-one degrees with mixed skies, ideal for peak foliage observation.\n\nFor optimal weekend viewing, Southeast Lower's Irish Hills along US-12 offers peak color with favorable conditions. Alternatively, Southwest Lower's Blue Star Highway near Saugatuck presents near-complete fall display with similar weather stability. Those in central regions might explore Chippewa Nature Center's river road in Midland, where forty-one percent color change and seventy-two-degree highs create comfortable conditions.",
